namespace Linx {

/**

\addtogroup creation

\section creation-intro Introduction

There are many ways to create arrays:

- From default values;
- Row by row, copying C arrays or ranges;
- By wrapping a data pointer;
- Through builtin functions like `fill()` and `linspace()`;
- Through generators with `generate()`;
- As the result of a transfrom: arithmetic operation, mathematical function, filter...

\section creation-default Default

Basic `Sequence` and `Image` constructors default-initialize the elements.

\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp default

\section creation-rowwise From C arrays and ranges

Sequences can be created from any array-like object, including C arrays, using either the constructor or the `resize()` function.
The latter accepts a template parameter specifying the memory space of the sequence, defaulting to the host.

\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp resize

Image of up rank up to 3 can be created from brace-enclosed lists or C arrays representing rows using function `rowwise()`.
The data type is either explicit or deduced from the input data type.
Like `resize()`, `rowwise()` accepts a template parameter specifying the memory space of the image.


\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp rowwise

\section creation-wrap Pointer wrapping

`Sequence` and `Image` can serve as adaptors for C pointers, in which case they do not manage memory:
no allocation or deallocation is performed, and the pointer must remain valid throughout the lifetime of the container.
This is especially useful to enable signal or image processing for arbitrary containers.

\snippet{trimleft} creation_example.cpp wrap

